---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Exporting to your own Immich server — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightPhotos can upload finished photos straight to any Immich server, even one on a box in your home. Here is how I made your server address and an API key work with no extra steps."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-26">September 26, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Exporting to your own Immich server</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          LightPhotos can upload finished photos straight to any Immich server,
          even one on a box in your home. Here is how I made your server address
          and an API key work with no extra steps.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Immich is a photo library that you host yourself. It is the nearest
          equivalent to Google Photos that you can run on your own hardware.
          The LightPhotos export form now has an Immich tab. Paste the address
          of your server and an API key, then click Connect. (An API key is a
          token, similar to a password, that your server gives you.)
          LightPhotos then sends your edited JPEGs to your server with their
          capture time and star rating.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of my work was on the tasks around the upload. LightPhotos must
          accept any address that you type, and it must check the key early.
          It must also cope with slow home internet. And it must never send a
          key unencrypted by accident. This feature is only in
          the desktop app, because browsers cannot talk to an Immich
          server.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Check a key with a request that needs the key.</strong>
          One hosted Immich service gave a reply to its "about this server"
          request for all keys. So a wrong key looked fine until
          the first upload failed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Let the server find duplicates.</strong> Immich already
          recognises a photo that it has seen before. A record of my own
          would be a second list, and that list could fall out of sync with the
          server.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Give larger uploads more time.</strong> One five-minute
          limit for each upload made large batches fail. This happened on
          connections that were slow but worked fine.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep the time zone with the time.</strong> Without the time
          zone, photos that I took at 6&nbsp;pm in California appeared in
          Immich at 11&nbsp;am.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why Immich</h2>
      <p>
        I keep my original photos in folders on a drive, and I will not change
        that. But nobody wants to browse a folder of JPEGs on a phone.
        Also, I do not want to give my photos to a service that I cannot leave.
        Immich sits between the two for me. It is a real photo library with
        a phone app. It runs on hardware that I own, or on a hosted service if
        I do not want to run it myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before this feature, I exported to a folder. Then I uploaded that
        folder by hand. Now this is one step.
      </p>

      <h2>Accept any address that you type</h2>
      <p>
        Each person has a server at a different address. Some people type
        <code>photos.example.com</code>, some paste
        <code>https://photos.example.com/api/</code> from the documentation,
        and some use <code>http://nas.local:2283</code> on their home network.
        I wanted all of these addresses to work.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 214" role="img" aria-label="How LightPhotos connects. First, it cleans the address. It removes spaces, a trailing slash and /api, and it adds https if there is no scheme. Then it reads the well-known discovery file of the server to find the API. If there is no discovery file, it uses /api. Then it checks the key: it asks the server who owns the key. LightPhotos saves the address and the key only if this check is successful.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">FROM A TYPED ADDRESS TO A CONNECTION</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="28" width="196" height="54" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="98" y="48" text-anchor="middle">1. Clean the address</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="98" y="64" text-anchor="middle">trim, drop trailing / and /api,</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="98" y="76" text-anchor="middle">add https:// if missing</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="196" y1="55" x2="212" y2="55"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M212 50 L222 55 L212 60 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="222" y="28" width="196" height="54" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="48" text-anchor="middle">2. Find the API</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="320" y="64" text-anchor="middle">/.well-known/immich</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="320" y="76" text-anchor="middle">if not found, use /api</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="418" y1="55" x2="434" y2="55"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M434 50 L444 55 L434 60 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="444" y="28" width="196" height="54" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="542" y="48" text-anchor="middle">3. Check the key</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="542" y="64" text-anchor="middle">GET /users/me</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="542" y="76" text-anchor="middle">"Connected as …"</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="542" y1="82" x2="542" y2="104"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M537 104 L547 104 L542 114 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="444" y="114" width="196" height="40" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="542" y="138" text-anchor="middle">Save address and key</text>

          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="6" cy="176" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="18" y="180">Incorrect key: "the server rejected the API key". LightPhotos saves nothing.</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="6" cy="200" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="18" y="204">No HTTPS: a hint to type http:// yourself. No silent change to HTTP.</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        First, LightPhotos cleans the address. It removes spaces, a trailing
        slash, or <code>/api</code>, and it adds <code>https://</code> if you
        did not type it. Then it reads the discovery file of the server,
        <code>/.well-known/immich</code>, to find the location of the API.
        (The API is the address that receives the requests from the app.)
        This helps people who host Immich under a sub-path, for example
        <code>/immich/api</code>. If there is no discovery file, LightPhotos
        uses the usual <code>/api</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sometimes you do not type <code>http://</code> or
        <code>https://</code>, and the secure HTTPS connection fails. In that
        case, LightPhotos does not quietly fall back to plain HTTP. That
        change would send your API key across the network without encryption,
        and it would not tell you. Instead, the error message tells you to
        start the address with <code>http://</code> if your server does not
        have HTTPS. Then the decision is yours.
      </p>

      <h2>Check the key early</h2>
      <p>
        The usual method to test a connection is to ask the server for
        information about itself. I tried that method on Gumnut, a hosted
        Immich service. Gumnut gave a reply for all keys, also for a key that
        I invented. So LightPhotos asks "who owns this key?" instead. Only
        a real key gets a reply to that question. The reply also gives the
        form a friendly message to show: "Connected as" with your name and
        email.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos saves nothing until that check is successful. Then it
        saves the address in the app settings, and it keeps the key in a
        safer location.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>On a Mac</strong>, the key goes into your login Keychain.
          There is one entry for each server.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On Linux</strong>, the key is in a file in the config folder
          of the app. Only your user can read this file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On Windows</strong>, the key is in a file in your own app
          data folder.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I am glad that I did one small detail correctly. LightPhotos does not
        access the Keychain until you open the Immich tab. If you never use
        Immich, you will never see a Keychain prompt.
      </p>

      <h2>What LightPhotos uploads</h2>
      <p>
        Immich gets your finished photo. LightPhotos renders the photo the
        same as for a folder export, with your edits, crop, and size. Then it
        saves the photo as a JPEG. Your original files stay on your drive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two items of information go with the photo:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The capture time.</strong> My first version read the clock
          of the camera as UTC (universal time). So a photo taken at
          6&nbsp;pm in California appeared in Immich at 11&nbsp;am. Now each
          export sends its capture time and the time-zone offset of the
          camera. If the camera did not record a capture time, LightPhotos
          uses the date of the file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your star rating.</strong> LightPhotos sets the rating
          immediately after the upload. Some keys have permission to upload
          but not to change photos. With such a key, the photo still uploads,
          and you get a warning about the rating. The export does not fail.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Running the same batch twice</h2>
      <p>
        Sometimes an export stops before it is complete. The laptop goes to
        sleep, or the Wi-Fi drops. The easiest fix is to do the
        export again, so that action must be safe.
      </p>
      <p>
        Luckily, Immich already handles this. If you upload the same
        file twice, Immich replies "duplicate" and identifies the photo
        that it already has. LightPhotos keeps no list of its uploads. It
        sends all the photos again, counts the duplicates, and shows a message
        such as "Uploaded 40 photos, 32 already on the server." The batch
        picks up where it stopped. I did not need to write any bookkeeping
        code for it.
      </p>

      <h2>Slow uploads that still work</h2>
      <p>
        Home internet connections are usually slow for uploads. Also,
        LightPhotos exports several photos at the same time. My first version
        gave each upload a fixed limit of five minutes. I tried a batch of
        large photos on a slow connection that eight uploads shared. The
        uploads hit that limit, although the connection worked fine.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now each upload gets one minute plus extra time that depends on its
        size. LightPhotos calculates the extra time for a 1&nbsp;Mbit/s
        connection that eight uploads share:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">time allowed = 60 s + photo size / 16 KB per second</pre>
      <p>
        So a 25&nbsp;MB photo gets approximately 28 minutes. This time is
        long, but a connection that is truly stuck still fails. The other
        limits stay short, for example the limit to connect and the limit to
        wait for a reply from the server.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it is not in the browser</h2>
      <p>
        The web version of LightPhotos shows the Immich tab with a note. The
        note tells you that the tab only works in the desktop app. A web
        page can talk to a server on a different site only if that
        server gives permission. Immich does not give this permission. So
        the browser blocks the request before it sends it. The desktop app is
        not a web page, so that rule does not apply to it.
      </p>

      <h2>What is next</h2>
      <p>
        My next task is album support. You will select an existing album or
        type the name of a new album, and the batch will go into it. After
        that, I want automatic retries for uploads that fail before they are
        complete. Servers with self-signed certificates are common on home
        networks, and they probably will not connect yet. If you have such a
        server, please tell me if it works for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you use Immich and something in this post does not fit your
        setup, please tell me on the <a
        href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">issue
        tracker</a>. The desktop builds are on the <a
        href="/downloads.html">downloads page</a>.
      </p>

